    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shoshone"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shoshone.jpg" alt="Shoshone" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshone</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shoshone_group_American_Indian_Mongoloid.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cyrus Thomas, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The people known to outsiders as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> call themselves Nʉmʉnʉʉ, “the People”; the name Comanche comes from a Ute word usually translated as “enemy”. Their ancestors were Shoshone-speaking bands of the Great Basin and the northern Plains in present-day Wyoming, and their language, of the Numic branch of the Uto-Aztecan family, became distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshone</a> around 1700. At about that time some of these bands, allied with the Utes, moved south-east towards the southern Plains, where bison were plentiful and horses, spreading from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680, could be had by trade and raiding. Spanish records first mention them in 1706, when officials in New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> feared an attack on Taos by Utes and Comanches. Over the following decades the Comanche became mounted bison hunters and drove the Jicarilla, Lipan and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> groups from the grasslands east of the Rocky Mountains. In 1746 French traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> helped to arrange an alliance with the Wichita villages to the east, a source of French goods and guns, and around the middle of the century the Comanche broke with their Ute allies. The territory they came to control, known in Spanish as Comanchería, stretched from the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> River to the Balcones Escarpment near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a> and from the Cross Timbers to the Pecos River.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> life rested on the horse and the bison. Bands moved with their herds across the grasslands, living in tipis covered with sewn buffalo hides that women made, raised and struck, and also did most of the butchering, meat-drying and tanning. A man’s wealth was measured by the size of his horse herd, and some men owned hundreds of animals. Meat, robes, horses and mules were exchanged for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, bread, metal tools, cloth and guns: at the trade fairs of Taos, with the New Mexican traders known as Comancheros, who came onto the Plains after the peace of 1786, with the Wichita villages and, from the 1790s, with Anglo-American traders, so that Comanche horses passed through other hands to markets as far away as St Louis. Raiding was part of the same economy, as horses, mules and captives taken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> entered the trade. Many captives, especially children, were adopted and raised as Comanche; others worked as herders or were sold or ransomed. Religious life centred on <em>puha</em>, the power a person could gain through visions, and on the cures and ceremonies of medicine men, and the Comanche language became the common language of trade across the southern Plains. Estimates of the population in the late eighteenth century are around 20,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-de-anza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-de-anza.jpg" alt="Juan Bautista de Anza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-de-anza">Juan Bautista de Anza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Juan_Bautista_de_Anza_(Painted_by_Fray_Orci;_1774,_Mexico_City).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fray Orci, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> military strength rested on horsemanship learned in childhood, on the lance, the bow and the buffalo-hide shield, and increasingly on guns obtained in trade. There was no standing army: any man who could recruit volunteers led a war party, and expeditions of several hundred riders could strike hundreds of miles from home. In March 1758 about 2,000 Comanches and allied warriors attacked the Spanish mission of Santa Cruz de San Sabá in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, founded for the Lipan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>, killing eight people, including two priests, and in 1759 Comanche and Wichita fighters repulsed Colonel Diego Ortiz Parrilla’s punitive expedition at the fortified Taovaya villages on the Red River. In 1779 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-de-anza">Juan Bautista de Anza</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, with Ute, Apache and Pueblo auxiliaries, surprised and killed the war leader Cuerno Verde in present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>. In the nineteenth century, raiders crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, wintered in the Bolsón de Mapimí and struck as far south as Durango, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>. After Texans killed Comanche leaders at a council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a> in March 1840, Buffalo Hump led a great raid to the coast at Linnville, and Texan forces attacked the returning column at Plum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>. Later, Texas Rangers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army carried the war into Comancheria itself, killing the leader Iron Jacket near the Antelope Hills in 1858.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Comancheria had no capital, no single ruler and no written law. Its basic unit was the local band, a group of related families whose headman, the <em>paraibo</em>, led by persuasion, generosity and success rather than command; a man who organised a raid was war leader only for that expedition. Bands belonged to larger divisions. Spanish officials before the 1750s dealt with three, the Jupes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamparika">Yamparikas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotsoteka">Kotsotekas</a>, while nineteenth-century Texans and Americans knew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penateka">Penateka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nokoni">Nokoni</a>, Kotsoteka, Yamparika and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwahadi">Kwahadi</a>, among others, each with its own chiefs and range. Councils of leading men discussed war, peace and the movement of camps and sought agreement, but individuals and bands who disagreed could go their own way, so a treaty bound only those who accepted it. Divisions could nonetheless act together. In 1785 a council of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanches</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> River chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecueracapa">Ecueracapa</a> to negotiate for them, and in February 1786 he made peace with Governor Anza at Santa Fe and Pecos, a settlement that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sustained with gifts and trade and that included an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, Governor Domingo Cabello had made peace with the eastern Comanches in 1785. Later the Republic of Texas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> negotiated with particular divisions and chiefs, at Tehuacana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> in 1844 and at Medicine Lodge in 1867.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo.jpg" alt="Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TreatyOfGuadalupeHidalgoCover.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Governments of USA and Mexico, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than a century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> were the leading power of the southern Plains, and they shaped the frontiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and Texas never expanded far onto the Plains, and after 1786 New Mexico became a trading partner and ally of the Comanche. Comanche raiding, together with that of the Kiowa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>, devastated much of northern Mexico in the 1830s and 1840s; one count of the larger raids between 1831 and 1848 records more than 2,600 Mexicans and at least 700 Comanches killed. The historian Brian DeLay has argued that this destruction left northern Mexico less able to resist the United States in the war of 1846–1848, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a> bound the United States to stop raids across the new border, an obligation dropped in 1853. Pekka Hämäläinen’s <em>The Comanche Empire</em> (2008) presented Comancheria as an indigenous empire built on trade, tribute and the control of horses and captives, an interpretation that won the Bancroft Prize and has been widely debated. The Comanche Nation, based at Lawton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, has about 17,000 enrolled citizens, and in 1944 fourteen Comanche code talkers landed in Normandy with the United States 4th Infantry Division.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/red-river-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-river-war.jpg" alt="Red River War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/red-river-war">Red River War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ledger-sm2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Comancheria was worn down by disease, ecological crisis and the expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a> struck in 1817 and 1848 and cholera in 1849, and the population fell from about 20,000 to a few thousand by the 1870s. Drought and shrinking bison herds in the 1850s undermined the economy of the hunt. After Texas joined the United States in 1845 the army built lines of forts, some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penateka">Penateka</a> settled on a reservation on the Clear Fork of the Brazos in 1854 and were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> in 1859. In the Medicine Lodge Treaty of October 1867, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> and Kiowa leaders, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamparika">Yamparika</a> chief Ten Bears, accepted a reservation of about three million acres in south-western Indian Territory, but many bands stayed on the Plains. When commercial hide hunters began to destroy the southern herd, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanah-parker">Quanah Parker</a> and the prophet Isatai led an attack on the hunters’ post at Adobe Walls in June 1874, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-war">Red River War</a>. On 28 September 1874 Colonel Ranald Mackenzie’s troops destroyed camps in Palo Duro Canyon and captured about 1,400 horses, most of which were shot. Hungry and without horses, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwahadi">Kwahadi</a> band under Quanah surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-sill">Fort Sill</a> in June 1875. The reservation was later broken into individual allotments, and its remaining land was opened to settlers in 1901.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Comancheria, the domain of the Nʉmʉnʉʉ, was for more than a century the dominant power of the southern Plains: its bands controlled trade and war from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> River deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, checked Spanish and Mexican expansion and kept their independence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> until 1875.</em></p>

